# Nuxel microSD auto-mount rule (S10+ / beyond2lte).
#
# Install to /etc/udev/rules.d/90-nuxel-microsd.rules.
#
# ONE JOB: when an EXTERNAL removable card appears, ask the auto-mount service to
# mount it READ-ONLY. Nothing here formats, partitions, repairs or writes a card.
#
# KERNEL=="mmcblk[1-9]*" is deliberate: the microSD is an external card
# (mmcblk1/2), while mmcblk0 is the INTERNAL UFS. The tool refuses mmcblk0 and
# /dev/block/* on its own as well; matching only mmcblk[1-9] here means the
# internal device is never even handed to a mount path.
#
# The event is handed to systemd (TAG+="systemd" / SYSTEMD_WANTS) rather than run
# inline, so a slow card cannot stall the udev event queue, and the unit is
# oneshot with Restart=no - a card that fails to mount is reported, never looped.
#
# The same "add" uevent is replayed for a card that was already seated when the
# kernel enumerated it, so the service is NOT a boot-time auto-mounter: the tool
# refuses to mount inside the boot window (uptime < automount.boot_grace_s) and
# reports the skip. diagnostics.auto_mount_on_boot is false and stays false.
#
# Deliberately NOT used as a condition: ID_DRIVE_FLASH_SD. It is not reliably set
# for mmc devices on this platform, and a rule that silently never fires would be
# worse than one that lets the tool decide. The tool does the real check
# (removable flag + external device + never mmcblk0).

ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="block", KERNEL=="mmcblk[1-9]", ENV{DEVTYPE}=="disk", \
  TAG+="systemd", ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}+="nuxel-microsd-automount.service"

# Partition devices of the same card: the tool mounts the partition node itself,
# so these only get a journal note - never a second mount attempt, never a
# filesystem change.
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="block", KERNEL=="mmcblk[1-9]p*", \
  RUN{program}+="/usr/bin/logger -t nuxel-microsd 'partition node %k appeared; the auto-mount service drives the mount'"
